WEBVTT

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:01.680
DANIEL ROTH: Hi, everyone.

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:05.100
I'm Daniel Roth, Principal
Product Manager for ASP.NET Core

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.990
and Blazor on the.NET team.

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:12.180
Welcome to Building for the
Agentic Web with.NET 11.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:15.750
The demands on modern web
apps, they just keep going up.

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:19.260
Users want more performance,
airtight security,

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:23.400
and full observability into how
their apps behave in production.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.130
Apps need to be modern
and interactive.

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:28.650
Most are cloud-native
and distributed,

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:31.800
and increasingly they need
agentic capabilities,

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.530
apps that reason, use
tools, and collaborate

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:36.420
with people in real time.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:39.780
Developers want to build more,
faster, which means building

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:43.290
with AI as a partner, and
the platforms underneath all

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:46.890
of this need to constantly
improve to keep up.

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:49.650
In.NET 11, we're investing
to address the needs

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:52.590
of the most demanding
modern web apps.

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:55.710
Earlier this year, we published
our ASP.NET Core roadmap

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:57.570
for.NET 11 on GitHub.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:01.110
You can find it at
aka.ms/aspnet/roadmap.

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:03.150
Everything we're doing
this release is organized

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:05.430
around these six themes.

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:08.250
First, address top
feedback and pain points.

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.830
Your feedback helps us
constantly improve the

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:11.910
.NET platform.

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:14.820
The issues you open,
the comments you post,

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:19.080
the thumbs-up reactions, they
directly drive what we work on.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:21.780
We're addressing as many
top-voted issues this release

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.430
as we can.

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:25.350
Strengthen the foundation.

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:28.290
Performance, security,
observability,

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:30.180
these underpin everything we do

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:34.500
and are what make.NET a
platform that you can trust.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.660
.NET runs some of the biggest
services in the world

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:42.000
at Microsoft, like Entra, Bing,
Teams, Xbox, and most of Azure,

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.100
and the demands of
those workloads push us

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.690
to keep raising the bar.

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:48.330
As a result, everyone benefits.

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:50.790
Next, invest in
our modern stack.

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:53.490
Minimal APIs, SignalR, Blazor,

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:55.560
these are where we're
actively innovating

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.390
and adding new capabilities

00:01:57.390 --> 00:02:00.780
to help you build
great modern web apps.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.150
Simplify building
distributed web apps.

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:05.940
Modern web apps are
distributed and cloud-ready.

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:08.370
We're working closely with our
friends on the Aspire team

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:11.580
so that building, running, and
deploying multi-service apps

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:12.990
that integrate with a variety

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:16.950
of cloud services feels
easy and natural.

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:19.410
Enable building
agentic web apps.

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:23.220
This includes integration with
the Microsoft Agent Framework,

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:27.540
the MCP C-Sharp SDK and
supporting new UI patterns

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:29.460
for AI-driven experiences.

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:32.070
We want ASP.NET Core and
Blazor to be the best way

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:33.930
to build agentic web apps.

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:34.830
And then finally,

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:36.810
Copilot-assisted
web development.

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:40.290
We want AI coding assistance to
love working with.NET as much

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:43.640
as you do, through agent
skills, custom agents,

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:46.250
tools and evaluation test suites

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:49.490
that measure real-world
coding agent effectiveness.

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:51.920
Let's dive into each
of these themes.

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:55.920
First, let's look at how we're
strengthening the foundation.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:57.630
Fundamentals are
something that we focus

00:02:57.630 --> 00:02:59.550
on with every.NET release,

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:03.390
because that's what makes.NET a
platform that you can count on,

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:04.950
and the great thing
is, you get many

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:07.080
of these improvements
just by upgrading.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:08.670
First of all, performance.

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:12.420
We try to make every release
of.NET the fastest release ever.

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:16.470
This release, we're reducing TLS
handshake overhead in Kestrel

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:18.720
so short-lived
connections are faster.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:21.540
We're also optimizing how we
handle malformed requests

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:24.390
so you can shed bad traffic
efficiently and free

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:26.850
up that capacity for
your real users.

00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:28.890
We've added Z standard
compression support,

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:31.800
which gives you broadly
level compression ratios

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:33.750
at much lower CPU cost.

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:37.230
And we're working with the
runtime team on runtime async,

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:40.050
a more efficient async
implementation to make sure

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:43.620
that it works great across the
platform, including for web.

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:45.030
Next, security.

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:46.710
We're hardening Kestrel,

00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:49.740
our cross-platform
high-performance web server,

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:51.180
and running security scans

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:53.310
on our code base using
the latest AI models

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:56.520
to ensure our platform is
secure and trustworthy.

00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:00.330
We're modernizing our cross-site
request forgery support using

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:04.020
fetch metadata headers like
fetch site and fetch mode based

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:06.660
on the latest OWASP
recommendations.

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:09.930
And for long-lived connections,
like you have with SignalR

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:13.230
and Blazor Server, we're also
adding automatic auth token

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:15.600
refresh, a long-standing request

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:18.450
that closes a real
usability gap.

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:20.250
And then finally
for observability,

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:23.250
ASP.NET Core now emits
OpenTelemetry semantic

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:25.040
convention tags natively.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:27.770
Every request is automatically
traced using the OTel

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:32.040
conventions without requiring
external instrumentation.

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:34.170
And we're working on full
OpenTelemetry support

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:37.050
for Blazor WebAssembly so
that component-level metrics

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:40.440
and traces from the browser
show up in the same dashboards

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:42.180
as your server telemetry.

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:44.880
The end result is that when
you upgrade to.NET 11,

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:48.800
your apps run faster, safer,
and are more observable.

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:51.480
No code changes required.

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:54.690
Next, our modern stack.

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:58.050
We're focusing our ASP.NET
Core investments in.NET 11

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:02.430
on our modern stack, minimal
APIs, SignalR, and Blazor.

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:03.900
That's where we're
pushing boundaries

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:05.640
and adding new capabilities.

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:09.180
Other parts of ASP.NET Core
like MVC, Razor Pages,

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:12.030
API controllers, they'll of
course continue to be supported

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:14.910
and will benefit from the
foundational improvements

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:16.320
that I mentioned earlier.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.540
But that's not really
where we're innovating.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:20.970
Our investments are focused
on our modern web stack

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:25.290
that most clearly aligns
with modern web app needs.

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:27.870
For minimal APIs, and also
for Blazor in this case,

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:30.030
we're adding support
for async validation

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:32.820
so that your validation logic
can perform long-running

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:34.440
operations without blocking,

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:37.470
like checking a database
or external service.

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:41.040
We now support the latest
OpenAPI version, OpenAPI 3.2,

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:44.310
with proper support for
binary file responses.

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:47.220
And we're fixing a long-standing
testing pain point

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:49.860
when using Web Application
Factory with minimal APIs

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:52.650
and trying to set up
custom configuration.

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:54.780
SignalR is getting
off-token refresh support,

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:57.870
like I mentioned earlier,
for long-lived connections,

00:05:57.870 --> 00:06:00.630
and we're also adding
correlation ID support

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:02.610
for distributed tracing.

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:04.740
Blazor has a lot happening.

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:07.500
We're filling out Blazor's
server-side rendering model

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:09.840
so that you can use static
server-side rendering

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:11.460
without compromise.

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:13.350
We're adding support
for TempData,

00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:16.950
binding to session state, output
caching, client validation

00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:18.870
without requiring interactivity,

00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:21.180
adding async validation,
and much more.

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:22.440
We're trying to
make it easier also

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:25.470
to scale your Blazor server
apps using pause and resume

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:27.600
so that you can free
up those resources

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:30.330
when they're being
held by idle circuits.

00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:33.540
On WebAssembly, the big
story is our runtime

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:35.100
consolidation effort.

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:37.770
We're transitioning the.NET
WebAssembly runtime

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:40.230
from mono to core CLR.

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:41.820
We'll preview that in.NET 11

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:44.430
and then ship it
stable in.NET 12.

00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:48.210
New runtime capabilities like
multi-threading, 64-bit memory,

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:51.720
and all the other Wasm
3.0 features will land

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:53.640
on the core CLR-based runtime,

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:55.710
so we don't have to
build them twice.

00:06:55.710 --> 00:06:59.130
In the meantime, we are also
shipping a new project template

00:06:59.130 --> 00:07:01.260
for Blazor Web Workers
that allow you

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:05.610
to move CPU-intensive work
off of the UI thread today.

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:08.040
And lastly, support
for C-Sharp unions.

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:10.680
Union support is coming
to C-Sharp finally,

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:14.280
and we're lighting it up across
the entire ASP.NET Core stack

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:18.210
for richer API contracts, more
expressive component parameters,

00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:20.820
and better type safety.

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:22.350
All right, enough slides.

00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:24.000
Let me show you some of
these new improvements

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:27.930
that we've shipped in
the.NET 11 previews so far.

00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:29.550
So here I have some
sample applications

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:33.000
that demonstrate a lot of the
new functionality in.NET 11.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:35.820
First, let's look at some of the
new minimal API improvements.

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:37.320
Here in this minimal
API project,

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:41.010
you can see that right
here, we've got support

00:07:41.010 --> 00:07:44.640
for OpenAPI 3.2 wired up and
configured, ready to go.

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:46.800
We can see that if we go
ahead and start this app

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:50.340
and get it running -- actually,

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:55.740
let's make sure we actually
start the API project.

00:07:55.740 --> 00:07:56.880
All right, great.

00:07:56.880 --> 00:07:58.710
And let's go ahead and
open this up in a browser

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:03.180
so we can just see
the OpenAPI document.

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:05.640
There we go, and if we zoom
in, you can see, yup, in fact,

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:07.770
this is using OpenAPI 3.2.

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:08.790
What does this give you?

00:08:08.790 --> 00:08:13.200
OpenAPI 3.2 adds support for
structured tags, better support

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:16.320
for streaming responses
like server-sent events,

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:19.380
and better representation
of different off-flows.

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:21.420
Also, if we look at what's
happening in the console,

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:24.120
we can see that we're currently
dumping to the console all

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:27.750
of the activity tags, and we
can see that we're getting,

00:08:27.750 --> 00:08:30.990
out of the box, OpenTelemetry
semantic conventions

00:08:30.990 --> 00:08:35.040
for the HTTP request, for
the response, for the route,

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:38.070
and that's without having to add
any additional instrumentation.

00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:40.740
If go back to the code for
the app, here we can see

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:43.650
that all we're doing is
adding the trace source

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:45.480
for Microsoft ASP.NET Core.

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:49.680
We don't have to add the
OpenTelemetry instrumentation

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:52.260
ASP.NET Core package anymore,
although you probably still want

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:54.240
to because it does
bring some niceties.

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:55.920
Hopefully, this native support

00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:58.950
for the OpenTelemetry semantic
conventions will just make it

00:08:58.950 --> 00:09:01.050
more efficient.

00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:05.310
Okay, so that's OpenTelemetry
and minimal APIs.

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:06.540
Let's look at some
Blazor features.

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:11.230
Let me switch this over
to Blazor and run this.

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:16.680
There we go.

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:19.650
Okay, so for Blazor, we got
all sorts of new goodies.

00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:24.900
A major focus for Blazor
in.NET 11 is giving you parity

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:27.540
with static server-side
rendering features

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:29.640
that you probably
are used to with MVC

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:31.950
but haven't been
available in Blazor yet.

00:09:31.950 --> 00:09:34.080
Let me go ahead and snap this
side by side with Visual Studio

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:37.250
so we can see these things
together with the code.

00:09:37.250 --> 00:09:38.360
Let's try and do like that.

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:42.110
Okay, so first of all,
environment boundary.

00:09:42.110 --> 00:09:44.330
This is a new component
that we've added in.NET 11

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:48.410
that lets you conditionally
render some content based off

00:09:48.410 --> 00:09:50.820
of the current ASP.NET
Core environment.

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:52.980
For example, you might want
to render some content only

00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:55.770
if you're in development or
in development and staging.

00:09:55.770 --> 00:09:58.680
You can also say that I want
to exclude this content, like,

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:01.380
don't render this content when
I'm, like, in production,

00:10:01.380 --> 00:10:02.970
and you can see the
corresponding content being

00:10:02.970 --> 00:10:05.400
rendered over here
in the application.

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:07.860
So that's the new environment
boundary component,

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:11.240
corresponds to the environment
tag helper in MVC.

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:12.920
We also have a new
label component

00:10:12.920 --> 00:10:15.350
that makes it much easier
for you to set up labels

00:10:15.350 --> 00:10:16.970
for your Blazor forms.

00:10:16.970 --> 00:10:19.860
Here's the label
component in action.

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:22.530
You can see that we're using
just a normal C-Sharp expression

00:10:22.530 --> 00:10:24.690
to point to the model
property that we want

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:26.520
to generate this label for,

00:10:26.520 --> 00:10:29.370
and that's generating us
a nice label in our form

00:10:29.370 --> 00:10:32.820
with a friendly display
name, properly associated

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:37.310
with its input
text HTML element.

00:10:37.310 --> 00:10:40.280
You can also, of course, use
the label component using the

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:42.530
wrapping pattern where
you wrap the input.

00:10:42.530 --> 00:10:44.610
That works, too.

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:47.340
Display names, a new component
that actually will give you

00:10:47.340 --> 00:10:50.310
that friendly name
for the property.

00:10:50.310 --> 00:10:53.220
Here in this table, you can see
we've got friendly names being

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:54.930
displayed for each column

00:10:54.930 --> 00:10:57.390
in this QuickGrid,
data grid component.

00:10:57.390 --> 00:11:01.350
If we look at the code for
that, that's right here.

00:11:01.350 --> 00:11:05.280
So here's where all those table
headings are being generated,

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:07.440
and they're being generated
using this new display name

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:10.530
component, which again, you just
pass in a C-Sharp expression

00:11:10.530 --> 00:11:12.420
and it will pull out
the friendly name

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:14.610
for that particular property.

00:11:14.610 --> 00:11:17.730
Down below, you can see that
we're also showing an example

00:11:17.730 --> 00:11:19.380
of using that display
name component

00:11:19.380 --> 00:11:21.060
for generating a label.

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:24.210
This is effectively what the
new label component is doing

00:11:24.210 --> 00:11:26.130
for you.

00:11:26.130 --> 00:11:28.290
QuickGrid has some new
bells and whistles as well.

00:11:28.290 --> 00:11:30.540
Let me make this a little
bit wider so we can see.

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:35.490
QuickGrid now has a new on-row
click component parameter

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:37.560
that you can use to wire
up an event handler

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:39.720
for when the user clicks
on a particular row

00:11:39.720 --> 00:11:41.640
and then gives you
the modeled instance

00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:43.260
that was used to
render that row.

00:11:43.260 --> 00:11:44.820
So if I click on
rows in this table,

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:47.100
you can see that it selects
the specific person

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:48.510
and then displays it.

00:11:48.510 --> 00:11:51.390
So that's pretty
nice, pretty cool.

00:11:51.390 --> 00:11:53.010
We don't have time to go through
all of these, but we'll get

00:11:53.010 --> 00:11:54.420
through as many as we can.

00:11:54.420 --> 00:11:57.870
TempData is a -- actually,
let's do the supply parameter

00:11:57.870 --> 00:11:59.430
from TempData version of this.

00:11:59.430 --> 00:12:01.560
TempData is a feature that
if you've used MVC before,

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:03.090
you're probably familiar with.

00:12:03.090 --> 00:12:05.940
It's where you want to save
a little piece of data

00:12:05.940 --> 00:12:10.200
so that it can be read again on
a subsequent page navigation.

00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:12.990
Often this is used when you
have a form, you do like a post

00:12:12.990 --> 00:12:15.780
and then a redirect get, you
want to save a little data

00:12:15.780 --> 00:12:18.930
to display on that
subsequent redirect.

00:12:18.930 --> 00:12:20.130
So you can now do
that in Blazor.

00:12:20.130 --> 00:12:21.360
We support TempData.

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:24.330
Like here, if I click on this
button, it'll do a post request

00:12:24.330 --> 00:12:26.850
and it'll save a little piece
of information, in this case,

00:12:26.850 --> 00:12:30.150
the server time into a
message that can be read

00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:32.640
from the page when
it then renders.

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:34.710
If we look at the code for
what that looks like --

00:12:34.710 --> 00:12:37.440
let me bring this over to
the side and let's look

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:40.620
at the supply parameter
from form example.

00:12:40.620 --> 00:12:42.360
So here's our edit form,

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:46.080
and then there's the
submit button that's --

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:47.280
when you click on the button,

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:50.430
that actually will capture the
server time, the definition,

00:12:50.430 --> 00:12:51.750
we should be able to see that.

00:12:51.750 --> 00:12:54.420
So here you can see that we're
grabbing the server time,

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:57.270
and then we're saving it in
this status message property.

00:12:57.270 --> 00:12:58.260
We'll look at that
just a second.

00:12:58.260 --> 00:12:59.490
And then we do that navigation.

00:12:59.490 --> 00:13:00.990
So this was a full
page navigation.

00:13:00.990 --> 00:13:03.420
It's not an interactive
rendering scenario.

00:13:03.420 --> 00:13:04.770
We then scroll back above.

00:13:04.770 --> 00:13:09.180
We can see that that status
message property has this new

00:13:09.180 --> 00:13:12.150
supply parameter from TempData
attribute, which is saying

00:13:12.150 --> 00:13:15.060
that this parameter should
be saved in TempData

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:16.770
and then made available,

00:13:16.770 --> 00:13:19.140
read out of TempData
when the page loads.

00:13:19.140 --> 00:13:22.770
That's how we're able to
then render that message.

00:13:22.770 --> 00:13:24.570
If we then refresh the page,
the TempData, of course,

00:13:24.570 --> 00:13:26.850
goes away because it's
just a temporary place

00:13:26.850 --> 00:13:29.430
to store some information,
usually stored in a cookie,

00:13:29.430 --> 00:13:32.610
although you can use
other providers as well.

00:13:32.610 --> 00:13:34.500
That's new TempData support.

00:13:34.500 --> 00:13:38.040
There's some new enhancements
to virtualization.

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:39.630
Let me show this example.

00:13:39.630 --> 00:13:42.210
So here we are using the
virtualized component

00:13:42.210 --> 00:13:44.400
to render a very
long list of items.

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:46.770
I think it's -- in this case,
it's like, yeah, 10,000 items

00:13:46.770 --> 00:13:48.510
that we're rendering
in this list.

00:13:48.510 --> 00:13:50.040
Virtualized is a nice component

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:53.640
that lets you just display
a long list of stuff,

00:13:53.640 --> 00:13:55.770
but only have to
render the parts

00:13:55.770 --> 00:13:58.410
that are currently visible,

00:13:58.410 --> 00:14:01.110
and you just basically create
a virtualized component

00:14:01.110 --> 00:14:02.640
and provide it the items
you want to render

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:04.500
and then how you want
them to be rendered.

00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:07.080
Previously, you had to
specify a fixed item size,

00:14:07.080 --> 00:14:08.970
like a fixed height for each
item, so it could figure

00:14:08.970 --> 00:14:11.070
out which items were
actually visible.

00:14:11.070 --> 00:14:13.440
Now it does the even more
complicated logic of figuring

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:15.030
out how to render
the right items

00:14:15.030 --> 00:14:16.890
when they even have
different heights,

00:14:16.890 --> 00:14:18.750
as you can see here
in this example.

00:14:18.750 --> 00:14:21.270
So we're rendering 10,000
rows really efficiently

00:14:21.270 --> 00:14:22.590
and very fast.

00:14:22.590 --> 00:14:23.760
Very nice.

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:27.570
And now a couple other
improvements with validation.

00:14:27.570 --> 00:14:30.150
So on this page,
again, another example

00:14:30.150 --> 00:14:32.850
of where we're improving
static server-side rendering,

00:14:32.850 --> 00:14:35.430
this is a form where we have
client-side validation logic.

00:14:35.430 --> 00:14:38.130
Like, if I go and -- let's
try and type my name,

00:14:38.130 --> 00:14:39.930
but I only put a single
character, a tab off,

00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:43.770
you can see that it validates,
but unlike other Blazor forums

00:14:43.770 --> 00:14:46.680
in the past, this page
is not interactive.

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:51.150
And if we open up the
browser dev tools

00:14:51.150 --> 00:14:53.790
and let's clear all filters

00:14:53.790 --> 00:14:55.320
and just refresh the
page one more time

00:14:55.320 --> 00:14:57.150
so we can see what's happening,

00:14:57.150 --> 00:14:59.850
if we look at the WebSocket
connections on this page,

00:14:59.850 --> 00:15:02.610
there is a WebSocket, but that's
just this browser refresh thing

00:15:02.610 --> 00:15:03.330
from Visual Studio.

00:15:03.330 --> 00:15:05.460
There's no Blazor
server WebSocket.

00:15:05.460 --> 00:15:08.010
There's also no WebAssembly
being loaded on this page.

00:15:08.010 --> 00:15:10.500
It's just a normal static
server-side rendered page,

00:15:10.500 --> 00:15:11.250
but we're still getting

00:15:11.250 --> 00:15:13.200
that rich client-side
validation support.

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:14.040
How is that happening?

00:15:14.040 --> 00:15:18.030
Well, Blazor now has built-in
client-side validation logic

00:15:18.030 --> 00:15:21.600
that can be run even when you're
running without interactivity.

00:15:21.600 --> 00:15:23.490
We have a new --

00:15:23.490 --> 00:15:26.010
a JavaScript-based validation
library that's incorporated

00:15:26.010 --> 00:15:28.770
with Blazor that enables these
scenarios so you don't fall off

00:15:28.770 --> 00:15:31.620
that cliff from going from
static server-side rendering

00:15:31.620 --> 00:15:34.050
to having to enable
interactivity just

00:15:34.050 --> 00:15:36.480
because you want some
validation on your form.

00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:37.170
So that's pretty cool.

00:15:37.170 --> 00:15:39.030
It works also when
you send the message.

00:15:39.030 --> 00:15:40.290
It's a very lightweight
library, too.

00:15:40.290 --> 00:15:43.530
It has no jQuery dependency,
which is really nice,

00:15:43.530 --> 00:15:47.010
but if we look at how
it's being implemented

00:15:47.010 --> 00:15:48.450
like on the actual
elements here,

00:15:48.450 --> 00:15:52.320
you can see that it's using the
same data validation attributes

00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:56.160
that are used by MVC
and Razor Pages.

00:15:56.160 --> 00:15:59.940
If we then switch the language
for this form from English

00:15:59.940 --> 00:16:03.060
to Spanish, and then let's
trigger the validation again,

00:16:03.060 --> 00:16:05.940
you can see that now my
validation error messages are

00:16:05.940 --> 00:16:09.570
being rendered localized,
which is fantastic.

00:16:09.570 --> 00:16:12.360
So data annotation-based
validation messages now get

00:16:12.360 --> 00:16:17.190
localization, long-standing pain
point now addressed in.NET 11.

00:16:17.190 --> 00:16:20.490
And then lastly for validation,
I want to show this form.

00:16:20.490 --> 00:16:22.200
This form's a little bit -- oh,
we should probably switch back

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:26.580
to English so we can -- for us
English speakers out there.

00:16:26.580 --> 00:16:28.230
Refresh, great, okay.

00:16:28.230 --> 00:16:31.350
So here what I want to do is I
want to select like a username

00:16:31.350 --> 00:16:34.770
for an account, but this
username needs to be unique.

00:16:34.770 --> 00:16:36.780
If I select a username
that's already taken,

00:16:36.780 --> 00:16:38.100
then I should get a
validation error.

00:16:38.100 --> 00:16:40.830
Like let's say I try to
grab the admin username,

00:16:40.830 --> 00:16:42.120
that's almost certainly taken.

00:16:42.120 --> 00:16:45.540
Notice that I get a little bit
of UI experience there saying,

00:16:45.540 --> 00:16:47.190
hold on, I'm checking
the database to see

00:16:47.190 --> 00:16:50.370
if that username's taken
already, and it is.

00:16:50.370 --> 00:16:53.520
That was an asynchronous
validation check.

00:16:53.520 --> 00:16:55.920
It wasn't synchronous.

00:16:55.920 --> 00:16:57.870
It was a long-running
activity and we were able

00:16:57.870 --> 00:17:02.940
to even add some UI experience
as part of the check happening.

00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:05.400
If I then select a username that
maybe it's not already taken,

00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:07.320
like my favorite
username, then, yeah,

00:17:07.320 --> 00:17:09.030
that one's currently available.

00:17:09.030 --> 00:17:12.030
So if we look at the
code for this page,

00:17:12.030 --> 00:17:19.020
the async validation page here,
when we look at the edit form,

00:17:19.020 --> 00:17:20.850
we can see that there's
some nice new APIs

00:17:20.850 --> 00:17:22.950
that have been added
to the edit context.

00:17:22.950 --> 00:17:26.250
Like here's the edit context
that now has APIs for checking.

00:17:26.250 --> 00:17:27.810
Are we in the process
of validating?

00:17:27.810 --> 00:17:29.790
Is the validation
faulted in some way?

00:17:29.790 --> 00:17:32.340
And is validation
messages available?

00:17:32.340 --> 00:17:36.000
And if we look at the handle
submit implementation,

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:38.880
we can see that there is
now a validate async method

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:40.560
on edit context.

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:41.850
So this is part of the story.

00:17:41.850 --> 00:17:43.380
In.NET 11, we're also working

00:17:43.380 --> 00:17:46.080
on actually adding async
validation support

00:17:46.080 --> 00:17:50.190
to the system component model
data annotations, APIs as well,

00:17:50.190 --> 00:17:51.540
in the core libraries.

00:17:51.540 --> 00:17:53.750
That's still coming, but once
that's in, you'll be able

00:17:53.750 --> 00:17:55.040
to just annotate your types

00:17:55.040 --> 00:17:57.800
with asynchronous validation
attributes and have that plug

00:17:57.800 --> 00:17:59.790
into this experience, too.

00:17:59.790 --> 00:18:00.480
Okay? Cool.

00:18:00.480 --> 00:18:01.020
So those are some

00:18:01.020 --> 00:18:03.660
of the server-based
Blazor improvements.

00:18:03.660 --> 00:18:04.770
What about WebAssembly?

00:18:04.770 --> 00:18:07.020
Well, there are WebAssembly
improvements as well.

00:18:07.020 --> 00:18:11.230
Let me close that up
and let's switch.

00:18:12.390 --> 00:18:13.740
Let's shut this down.

00:18:13.740 --> 00:18:16.770
Okay, so we also -- and you
can see in the solution,

00:18:16.770 --> 00:18:19.980
we have a Blazor, a standalone
Blazor WebAssembly app,

00:18:19.980 --> 00:18:23.630
and then we also have a new
Blazor Web Worker project.

00:18:23.630 --> 00:18:27.260
So Blazor Web Worker is a
way to run some.NET code

00:18:27.260 --> 00:18:30.140
on WebAssembly in the browser,
but not on the UI thread.

00:18:30.140 --> 00:18:31.760
Have it run in a
separate Web Worker.

00:18:31.760 --> 00:18:32.630
If you want to create one,

00:18:32.630 --> 00:18:35.000
there's a new project
template you can use.

00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:37.980
You just search here
for Web Worker.

00:18:37.980 --> 00:18:38.670
We should find it.

00:18:38.670 --> 00:18:40.260
Yup, there it is.

00:18:40.260 --> 00:18:41.880
So this guy right here.

00:18:41.880 --> 00:18:45.480
That's the new Blazor Web Worker
project template that I used

00:18:45.480 --> 00:18:48.120
for this sample application,
and it's just reference

00:18:48.120 --> 00:18:53.730
from the Blazor Wasm
Features project,

00:18:53.730 --> 00:18:55.950
which is just a standalone
Blazor WebAssembly app.

00:18:55.950 --> 00:18:59.370
And what this gives you is it
gives you this Web Worker client

00:18:59.370 --> 00:19:02.630
class that you can use to
create Web Worker clients,

00:19:02.630 --> 00:19:05.150
and that will then run a bunch
of JavaScript, which will set

00:19:05.150 --> 00:19:07.580
up the.NET runtime to run

00:19:07.580 --> 00:19:11.040
in a Worker instead
of on the UI thread.

00:19:11.040 --> 00:19:13.530
And then you have this
InvokeAsync method

00:19:13.530 --> 00:19:17.190
that you can then use to
call some code that you want

00:19:17.190 --> 00:19:22.740
to have executed on
that Worker thread.

00:19:22.740 --> 00:19:28.130
So here in this app, I've got
this data worker class that's

00:19:28.130 --> 00:19:30.980
sitting in the Blazor
WebAssembly project,

00:19:30.980 --> 00:19:34.070
and it has this
AnalyzeSalesData method

00:19:34.070 --> 00:19:37.220
that you can see is labeled as a
JS export, so it can be called

00:19:37.220 --> 00:19:40.070
from that Web Worker, and this
just does a whole bunch of like,

00:19:40.070 --> 00:19:42.230
you know, compute, like it
takes a bunch of sales records

00:19:42.230 --> 00:19:43.790
and does a bunch of
number crunching on them,

00:19:43.790 --> 00:19:45.900
some CPU-intensive task.

00:19:45.900 --> 00:19:47.460
If we tried to do this
on the UI thread,

00:19:47.460 --> 00:19:49.290
it would probably
freeze the UI thread

00:19:49.290 --> 00:19:51.660
and prevent UI interactions,
but with the new Web Worker,

00:19:51.660 --> 00:19:57.690
we can push it off on to
a separate Web Worker

00:19:57.690 --> 00:19:59.760
so it doesn't block the flow.

00:19:59.760 --> 00:20:01.350
So let's see what that
looks like if we run it.

00:20:01.350 --> 00:20:07.030
We switch to Visualizing
Features and let's run this.

00:20:08.640 --> 00:20:11.220
Okay, so there's going two
variations of this app,

00:20:11.220 --> 00:20:14.390
one where we're going to run it
locally, like on the UI thread,

00:20:14.390 --> 00:20:16.550
and then one where we run
it on the Web Worker.

00:20:16.550 --> 00:20:19.100
So here you can see there's this
counter that's chugging away.

00:20:19.100 --> 00:20:21.230
That's showing us that
the UI thread is healthy.

00:20:21.230 --> 00:20:23.480
If I try to process like,
you know, a quarter million

00:20:23.480 --> 00:20:26.070
of the sales records
on the main thread,

00:20:26.070 --> 00:20:29.490
notice that the counter freezes,
things aren't running anymore.

00:20:29.490 --> 00:20:30.540
We've blocked threads.

00:20:30.540 --> 00:20:33.450
That's not great, but if we
instead choose to process it

00:20:33.450 --> 00:20:36.200
on a Web Worker, let's do that,

00:20:36.200 --> 00:20:38.150
notice that the counter
continues to chug along.

00:20:38.150 --> 00:20:39.320
We haven't blocked
the UI thread.

00:20:39.320 --> 00:20:42.560
We can still navigate around
the app, and we're able to do

00:20:42.560 --> 00:20:44.270
that processing, okay?

00:20:44.270 --> 00:20:46.820
Now, to be clear, this is not
like true multithreading.

00:20:46.820 --> 00:20:48.260
There's no shared memory here

00:20:48.260 --> 00:20:50.940
between the Web Worker
and your UI thread.

00:20:50.940 --> 00:20:53.280
You can think of it more
like a separate process.

00:20:53.280 --> 00:20:55.710
It's just message
passing between the two,

00:20:55.710 --> 00:20:59.670
and you are actually loading a
second.NET WebAssembly runtime

00:20:59.670 --> 00:21:02.640
in that Web Worker, so can
be a bit memory-heavy,

00:21:02.640 --> 00:21:05.280
so be judicious in how
you use this feature.

00:21:05.280 --> 00:21:07.200
But still, very cool
way for you to move

00:21:07.200 --> 00:21:10.290
that CPU-intensive work
off of the UI thread

00:21:10.290 --> 00:21:13.200
with the existing runtime.

00:21:13.200 --> 00:21:16.350
All right, those are some
of the new improvements

00:21:16.350 --> 00:21:21.420
that are already available
with.NET 11, the.NET 11 previews.

00:21:21.420 --> 00:21:25.050
All right, next, let's talk
about how we're making it easier

00:21:25.050 --> 00:21:30.030
to build distributed and
cloud-ready.NET web apps

00:21:30.030 --> 00:21:34.800
with Aspire, with our
friends on the Aspire team.

00:21:34.800 --> 00:21:37.410
Modern web apps are, of
course, distributed.

00:21:37.410 --> 00:21:40.500
You've typically got a front
end, you've got backend APIs,

00:21:40.500 --> 00:21:44.820
you have AI services that you're
talking to, databases, caches.

00:21:44.820 --> 00:21:46.530
That's a lot of moving parts.

00:21:46.530 --> 00:21:50.280
Aspire solves these development
and deployment concerns.

00:21:50.280 --> 00:21:53.730
It gives you orchestration,
observability,

00:21:53.730 --> 00:21:56.130
and configuration management
for distributed apps.

00:21:56.130 --> 00:21:58.470
You get a great local
development experience

00:21:58.470 --> 00:22:00.630
and a clean deployment story.

00:22:00.630 --> 00:22:03.720
In the.NET 11 timeframe,
we're deepening our Blazor

00:22:03.720 --> 00:22:05.520
and Aspire integration.

00:22:05.520 --> 00:22:08.940
We're making Blazor WebAssembly
work properly with Aspire

00:22:08.940 --> 00:22:11.310
so that you get configuration,
service discovery,

00:22:11.310 --> 00:22:13.830
OpenTelemetry, and
simplified debugging

00:22:13.830 --> 00:22:16.950
from the browser all the
way through to the backend.

00:22:16.950 --> 00:22:18.300
We're also integrating
with Aspire

00:22:18.300 --> 00:22:20.640
to simplify Blazor
server scaling.

00:22:20.640 --> 00:22:21.630
Now, to make all this work,

00:22:21.630 --> 00:22:23.850
we're adding a new
Blazor-specific Aspire host

00:22:23.850 --> 00:22:28.200
integration package and a new
Blazor gateway service that sits

00:22:28.200 --> 00:22:30.690
between the browser
and your backend.

00:22:30.690 --> 00:22:34.080
It serves your standalone
Blazor WebAssembly apps using

00:22:34.080 --> 00:22:37.560
MapStaticAssets, so gets all
the optimizations there.

00:22:37.560 --> 00:22:42.570
It also handles proxying API
calls to any backend services.

00:22:42.570 --> 00:22:44.430
So no need for cores.

00:22:44.430 --> 00:22:46.500
It will flow configuration data,

00:22:46.500 --> 00:22:50.190
like your service discovery
data, to the client,

00:22:50.190 --> 00:22:53.250
and it also collects
OpenTelemetry information

00:22:53.250 --> 00:22:55.800
for like populating
the Aspire dashboard,

00:22:55.800 --> 00:22:59.580
and it can handle a
session affinity as well.

00:22:59.580 --> 00:23:02.610
It replaces our existing Blazor
dev server that we've used

00:23:02.610 --> 00:23:04.740
for many years for local
development with something

00:23:04.740 --> 00:23:05.940
that is now production-grade.

00:23:05.940 --> 00:23:07.410
It's good for local development,

00:23:07.410 --> 00:23:10.050
but also when you deploy
the app, it can be used

00:23:10.050 --> 00:23:13.170
as a production service to
host your standalone Blazor

00:23:13.170 --> 00:23:16.500
WebAssembly apps with all the
appropriate optimizations

00:23:16.500 --> 00:23:17.970
and integrations.

00:23:17.970 --> 00:23:19.720
Let me show you what I mean.

00:23:22.980 --> 00:23:26.160
Okay, let me switch
out of this app.

00:23:26.160 --> 00:23:30.540
Let's look at a standalone
Blazor WebAssembly app

00:23:30.540 --> 00:23:33.780
that has now been integrated
fully with Aspire, okay?

00:23:33.780 --> 00:23:35.970
So here in this solution, we've
got a few things going on.

00:23:35.970 --> 00:23:38.610
We have a -- this is a
Blazor WebAssembly project,

00:23:38.610 --> 00:23:41.640
just a standalone project,
so no server component,

00:23:41.640 --> 00:23:45.420
and then we have a backend
API, like a minimal API

00:23:45.420 --> 00:23:46.340
for getting somewhat

00:23:46.340 --> 00:23:49.740
of the Blazor WebAssembly
frontend calls.

00:23:49.740 --> 00:23:51.300
And then we have some
