00:28:27.360 --> 00:28:29.760
That's also coming.

00:28:29.760 --> 00:28:33.960
All right, now let's talk about
making your web apps agentic.

00:28:33.960 --> 00:28:36.570
Modern web apps are
increasingly agentic.

00:28:36.570 --> 00:28:40.290
They include AI agents that
reason, plan, use tools,

00:28:40.290 --> 00:28:43.260
interact with -- and interact
with the user in real time.

00:28:43.260 --> 00:28:45.300
That's a new set of requirements
for a web framework,

00:28:45.300 --> 00:28:48.600
and we want ASP.NET Core and
Blazor to be the best place

00:28:48.600 --> 00:28:50.340
to build these kinds of apps.

00:28:50.340 --> 00:28:51.810
We've been working
closely with our friends

00:28:51.810 --> 00:28:53.490
on the Microsoft
Agent Framework team

00:28:53.490 --> 00:28:56.550
so that you can host agents
directly inside your ASP.NET

00:28:56.550 --> 00:29:00.630
Core apps and tap into the
capabilities of AI services

00:29:00.630 --> 00:29:02.640
like Microsoft Foundry.

00:29:02.640 --> 00:29:04.530
That includes built-in
hosting support

00:29:04.530 --> 00:29:07.530
for different agentic protocols,

00:29:07.530 --> 00:29:11.190
like the OpenAI responses
protocol for chat completions

00:29:11.190 --> 00:29:15.270
and function calling, the A to
A, or agent to agent protocol

00:29:15.270 --> 00:29:18.810
for multi-agent orchestration,
and also for AGUI,

00:29:18.810 --> 00:29:21.600
a protocol for real-time
agent to human

00:29:21.600 --> 00:29:24.270
or user interface interactions.

00:29:24.270 --> 00:29:26.640
You can expose your
app's functionality also

00:29:26.640 --> 00:29:30.480
as an MCP server to
other agentic apps

00:29:30.480 --> 00:29:33.540
or have your app act as a
client that can consume tools

00:29:33.540 --> 00:29:36.960
and resources from other
MCP servers using the MCP

00:29:36.960 --> 00:29:38.790
C-Sharp SDK.

00:29:38.790 --> 00:29:42.360
All this integrates naturally
with ASP.NET Core's middleware,

00:29:42.360 --> 00:29:43.530
dependency injection system,

00:29:43.530 --> 00:29:45.990
authentication,
and observability.

00:29:45.990 --> 00:29:50.760
Now, building agentic UIs
is its own challenge.

00:29:50.760 --> 00:29:54.540
You have streaming messages,
you want to show tool execution

00:29:54.540 --> 00:29:56.400
and reasoning and progress.

00:29:56.400 --> 00:29:58.740
You want to synchronize
state between your agent

00:29:58.740 --> 00:29:59.640
and the user interface

00:29:59.640 --> 00:30:01.620
so it knows what the
user is looking at.

00:30:01.620 --> 00:30:04.290
You might want to have
multimodal inputs like speech

00:30:04.290 --> 00:30:06.930
and images or video,
and you often want

00:30:06.930 --> 00:30:08.970
to have human-in-the-loop
workflows where the human

00:30:08.970 --> 00:30:12.600
and the agent work together and
the human stays in control.

00:30:12.600 --> 00:30:16.680
With the AGUI protocol, or agent
user interaction protocol,

00:30:16.680 --> 00:30:19.470
you get an open
event-driven protocol

00:30:19.470 --> 00:30:21.060
that standardizes
the wire format

00:30:21.060 --> 00:30:23.100
between agents and
user interfaces.

00:30:23.100 --> 00:30:26.760
AGUI lets you focus on your UI
instead of all that plumbing.

00:30:26.760 --> 00:30:29.490
AGUI support in Microsoft
Agent Framework is currently

00:30:29.490 --> 00:30:31.980
in preview, and we're also
looking to build a set

00:30:31.980 --> 00:30:34.980
of Blazor AI components
to make building agentic

00:30:34.980 --> 00:30:37.410
UIs straightforward.

00:30:37.410 --> 00:30:39.610
Let me show you what
that looks like.

00:30:42.600 --> 00:30:45.690
Now, if you're unfamiliar
with AGUI, I encourage you

00:30:45.690 --> 00:30:50.730
to learn more about it
by going to agui.com,

00:30:50.730 --> 00:30:53.640
which will bring you to the
AGUI specification page.

00:30:53.640 --> 00:30:56.490
They also have a really
nice sample application,

00:30:56.490 --> 00:31:00.750
which is this AGUI Dojo app,
which showcases a whole bunch

00:31:00.750 --> 00:31:03.960
of different AGUI
scenarios using a variety

00:31:03.960 --> 00:31:05.910
of different agentic
framework backends,

00:31:05.910 --> 00:31:07.830
including the Microsoft
Agent Framework.

00:31:07.830 --> 00:31:10.380
So you can see here that
you can do standard things

00:31:10.380 --> 00:31:12.870
like have chat interfaces,
like if we ask it

00:31:12.870 --> 00:31:15.030
to generate a sonnet,
it will, of course,

00:31:15.030 --> 00:31:18.390
then stream us back
a lovely sonnet.

00:31:18.390 --> 00:31:19.950
So pretty cool stuff,
and there's a bunch

00:31:19.950 --> 00:31:22.500
of other scenarios
showcased here.

00:31:22.500 --> 00:31:26.100
Now, this front end is built
using JavaScript and React.

00:31:26.100 --> 00:31:28.650
It's built using a set
of components provided

00:31:28.650 --> 00:31:31.170
by CopilotKit, which
you can absolutely use

00:31:31.170 --> 00:31:34.470
and might be a great fit
for your.NET backend,

00:31:34.470 --> 00:31:36.090
but what if you wanted
to build experiences

00:31:36.090 --> 00:31:37.980
like this with Blazor?

00:31:37.980 --> 00:31:39.930
Well, that's what
we've been working on.

00:31:39.930 --> 00:31:44.700
So here in this project, I
have a sample application

00:31:44.700 --> 00:31:49.860
that is a.NET and Blazor
implementation of the AGUI Dojo.

00:31:49.860 --> 00:31:52.650
Now, I'm going to take a second
to get this up and running,

00:31:52.650 --> 00:31:56.490
so let me go ahead and kick this
off so it starts to execute,

00:31:56.490 --> 00:31:58.080
and I'll show you
some of the code.

00:31:58.080 --> 00:32:00.930
So here in the server project,

00:32:00.930 --> 00:32:04.320
this is where we're hosting
our agents using Microsoft

00:32:04.320 --> 00:32:05.700
Agent Framework.

00:32:05.700 --> 00:32:06.450
You can see that in order

00:32:06.450 --> 00:32:10.770
to create Microsoft Agent
Framework AUI endpoint,

00:32:10.770 --> 00:32:13.770
all you have to do is call
MapAGUI, and then you just point

00:32:13.770 --> 00:32:16.110
at the agent that you
want to host there.

00:32:16.110 --> 00:32:18.300
You can see that we've
got a bunch of agents

00:32:18.300 --> 00:32:21.060
that we're wiring up that way.

00:32:21.060 --> 00:32:22.350
With Agent Framework, one

00:32:22.350 --> 00:32:24.780
of the cool things it does
is it integrates with --

00:32:24.780 --> 00:32:25.440
it's built on top

00:32:25.440 --> 00:32:27.720
of the Microsoft.Extensions.AI
abstractions

00:32:27.720 --> 00:32:30.930
so you can do things like
wire up support for tools.

00:32:30.930 --> 00:32:34.080
Like here, we have an
agent that has access

00:32:34.080 --> 00:32:37.440
to this GetWeather tool
so they can figure

00:32:37.440 --> 00:32:39.420
out what the current
weather is from the server.

00:32:39.420 --> 00:32:42.300
So it integrates nicely
with that functionality.

00:32:42.300 --> 00:32:47.610
Then on the client, we also
provide an AGUI chat client

00:32:47.610 --> 00:32:50.010
implementation, which
you can see right --

00:32:50.010 --> 00:32:51.720
like for example, right here.

00:32:51.720 --> 00:32:52.830
Here's an example of one.

00:32:52.830 --> 00:32:57.300
AGUI chat client is
actually an implementation

00:32:57.300 --> 00:33:00.990
of the iChat client interface
from Microsoft.Extensions.AI,

00:33:00.990 --> 00:33:04.050
so it plugs in seamlessly with
all of that infrastructure,

00:33:04.050 --> 00:33:05.790
but it also has
specific affordances

00:33:05.790 --> 00:33:09.270
for UI scenarios based
off of the AGUI protocol.

00:33:09.270 --> 00:33:10.890
Like for example, one of
the things you can do

00:33:10.890 --> 00:33:15.180
with AGUI is you can actually
have frontend tools,

00:33:15.180 --> 00:33:17.130
tools that aren't just
available from the server,

00:33:17.130 --> 00:33:19.860
but can also be available
from the client.

00:33:19.860 --> 00:33:23.250
Like down here, you can see
that we're setting up a tool

00:33:23.250 --> 00:33:25.230
that allows the agent,
if he wants to,

00:33:25.230 --> 00:33:27.660
to call down into the
client and change the color

00:33:27.660 --> 00:33:31.140
of the background in
the user interface.

00:33:31.140 --> 00:33:32.070
So let's check this out.

00:33:32.070 --> 00:33:33.450
Let's see if the
app up and running.

00:33:33.450 --> 00:33:35.070
Looks like it is.

00:33:35.070 --> 00:33:39.630
Let's now go back here and
let's run the laser version

00:33:39.630 --> 00:33:41.520
of the AGUI Dojo.

00:33:41.520 --> 00:33:42.960
We should be able to
still do the same things,

00:33:42.960 --> 00:33:47.230
like generate a sonnet and
get some streaming text back.

00:33:48.870 --> 00:33:49.710
There we go.

00:33:49.710 --> 00:33:51.540
So that looks good,
but what's really neat

00:33:51.540 --> 00:33:55.020
and different is we can
also ask you, like, hey,

00:33:55.020 --> 00:33:56.790
could you please change
the background color

00:33:56.790 --> 00:33:57.540
on the front end?

00:33:57.540 --> 00:34:00.720
And this is a frontend tool call
that then gets plumbed back

00:34:00.720 --> 00:34:04.140
to the agent so it can
talk back to the client

00:34:04.140 --> 00:34:05.670
and execute that tool.

00:34:05.670 --> 00:34:06.750
So that's pretty neat.

00:34:06.750 --> 00:34:08.820
You can, of course, call
backend tools as well,

00:34:08.820 --> 00:34:11.970
and AGUI has affordances for
rendering custom widgets

00:34:11.970 --> 00:34:13.200
when you invoke a tool.

00:34:13.200 --> 00:34:15.390
Like here, we're
rendering this widget

00:34:15.390 --> 00:34:16.380
for representing the weather

00:34:16.380 --> 00:34:20.460
when we're making a backend
tool call to that weather tool.

00:34:20.460 --> 00:34:22.350
You can do human-in-the-loop
scenarios.

00:34:22.350 --> 00:34:25.410
Like let's say we want to
work on a plan together

00:34:25.410 --> 00:34:28.820
with the agent, so here we're
getting a custom component

00:34:28.820 --> 00:34:31.100
that's being rendered where
we can select which parts

00:34:31.100 --> 00:34:33.680
of this plan we like, and then
we can either confirm, yup,

00:34:33.680 --> 00:34:36.050
that looks good, or we can
reject it and say, no, no,

00:34:36.050 --> 00:34:37.400
agent, I've changed my
mind, don't do that,

00:34:37.400 --> 00:34:39.870
and have it roll back.

00:34:39.870 --> 00:34:43.710
So that's how you can have,
like, human-agent interactions.

00:34:43.710 --> 00:34:46.340
And then you can also have flows

00:34:46.340 --> 00:34:49.490
that are long-running
and generative.

00:34:49.490 --> 00:34:51.920
Like here, if we ask the agent
for a plan and then we want it

00:34:51.920 --> 00:34:53.870
to execute it, like here, I
think it's generating a plan

00:34:53.870 --> 00:34:56.300
for going to Mars, you can see

00:34:56.300 --> 00:34:59.250
that I'm getting streamed
updates into the user interface

00:34:59.250 --> 00:35:01.950
as the agent makes progress
on this particular plan,

00:35:01.950 --> 00:35:04.380
because there are specific
events in the AGUI protocol

00:35:04.380 --> 00:35:06.300
that allow it to do
that, and much more.

00:35:06.300 --> 00:35:08.820
It can also, you know, you
can have full state sharing

00:35:08.820 --> 00:35:11.220
between the state of
the UI and the agent,

00:35:11.220 --> 00:35:13.500
so it can basically be
a full collaborator

00:35:13.500 --> 00:35:15.180
in your UI experience.

00:35:15.180 --> 00:35:18.240
These are just some of the
experiences that AGUI enables,

00:35:18.240 --> 00:35:22.320
and we are also working on
Blazor components specifically

00:35:22.320 --> 00:35:26.340
to make these scenarios easy
to build, and you can see some

00:35:26.340 --> 00:35:30.570
of these components here in
this sample application.

00:35:30.570 --> 00:35:32.250
Like, we have this agent
boundary component

00:35:32.250 --> 00:35:33.990
for the chat interface.

00:35:33.990 --> 00:35:38.460
You point to the agent
that you want to drive

00:35:38.460 --> 00:35:40.560
with this interface, and then
this messages component,

00:35:40.560 --> 00:35:42.570
these agent-loading
indicator components.

00:35:42.570 --> 00:35:44.910
Don't get too attached to
the specific names here.

00:35:44.910 --> 00:35:47.220
These are still
just a prototype,

00:35:47.220 --> 00:35:50.930
but it shows you the
direction that we're going,

00:35:50.930 --> 00:35:52.190
something to look forward to.

00:35:52.190 --> 00:35:55.250
All right, so that's how you can
build agentic user interfaces

00:35:55.250 --> 00:35:58.950
using.NET and Blazor with AGUI.

00:36:01.550 --> 00:36:05.690
Let's just stop
this application.

00:36:05.690 --> 00:36:06.990
There we go.

00:36:12.650 --> 00:36:14.990
All right, finally, let's talk

00:36:14.990 --> 00:36:18.440
about how we're improving
AI-assisted.NET

00:36:18.440 --> 00:36:19.860
web development.

00:36:19.860 --> 00:36:23.010
Now, AI coding
assistants, like Copilot,

00:36:23.010 --> 00:36:25.770
they can be a great partner when
they understand the frameworks

00:36:25.770 --> 00:36:28.680
that you're using, but sometimes
they struggle with features

00:36:28.680 --> 00:36:30.660
or scenarios that
they're unfamiliar with.

00:36:30.660 --> 00:36:32.430
They might pick
the wrong pattern,

00:36:32.430 --> 00:36:35.550
they might miss a new feature,
or they might generate something

00:36:35.550 --> 00:36:37.980
that compiles but
isn't idiomatic.

00:36:37.980 --> 00:36:39.900
So we're investing in four areas

00:36:39.900 --> 00:36:44.430
to make AI assistance
genuinely smarter about.NET.

00:36:44.430 --> 00:36:46.380
First of all, agentic skills.

00:36:46.380 --> 00:36:49.170
For our web, we're working
on a variety of ASP.NET Core

00:36:49.170 --> 00:36:53.430
and Blazor AI skills to give
coding agents useful new

00:36:53.430 --> 00:36:55.470
capabilities and expertise.

00:36:55.470 --> 00:36:58.920
You can find and install
these skills from the set

00:36:58.920 --> 00:37:01.350
of curated.NET plugins
that are available

00:37:01.350 --> 00:37:04.980
in the.NET Skills
repo and marketplace.

00:37:04.980 --> 00:37:06.840
We're also exploring
custom agents,

00:37:06.840 --> 00:37:10.410
which are like specialized
personas for.NET work.

00:37:10.410 --> 00:37:13.680
Like you might have a backend
ASP.NET Core developer

00:37:13.680 --> 00:37:15.810
or a frontend laser
developer, a tester,

00:37:15.810 --> 00:37:17.430
a doc writer, an architect.

00:37:17.430 --> 00:37:19.410
Each can have deep
framework knowledge

00:37:19.410 --> 00:37:22.050
and a focused skill set
for that specific role.

00:37:22.050 --> 00:37:24.570
We're exploring which
personas might matter most

00:37:24.570 --> 00:37:26.520
for.NET developers.

00:37:26.520 --> 00:37:30.960
Tools, we can give command
line tools and executables

00:37:30.960 --> 00:37:33.990
that give your agents
new capabilities.

00:37:33.990 --> 00:37:37.410
For example, the C-Sharp LSP
gives agents a rich semantic

00:37:37.410 --> 00:37:40.470
engine for navigating
and editing your code.

00:37:40.470 --> 00:37:43.560
The.NET Inspect command line
tool lets an agent query.NET

00:37:43.560 --> 00:37:46.860
APIs across NuGet packages
and local projects.

00:37:46.860 --> 00:37:49.260
Other MCP servers can
add useful features

00:37:49.260 --> 00:37:51.690
like integration
with Playwright.

00:37:51.690 --> 00:37:53.280
We're exploring what CLI

00:37:53.280 --> 00:37:57.660
and MCP-based tools should be
included for.NET scenarios.

00:37:57.660 --> 00:37:59.940
And then lastly,
eval test suites.

00:37:59.940 --> 00:38:04.440
We're building evaluation tests
for real.NET coding tasks

00:38:04.440 --> 00:38:06.870
so that we can actually
measure whether our skills,

00:38:06.870 --> 00:38:08.940
custom agents, and
tools move the needle

00:38:08.940 --> 00:38:10.710
and actually improve
the experience.

00:38:10.710 --> 00:38:11.790
We can't measure it.

00:38:11.790 --> 00:38:13.170
We don't really
know if it works.

00:38:13.170 --> 00:38:15.900
The eval suite helps
keep us honest.

00:38:15.900 --> 00:38:21.570
Now let me show you one of our
new Blazor skills in action.

00:38:21.570 --> 00:38:23.400
Pop over here.

00:38:23.400 --> 00:38:27.150
Let's open up this project.

00:38:27.150 --> 00:38:29.910
Actually, before we look
deeply at that project,

00:38:29.910 --> 00:38:34.740
let me show you the.NET
Skills marketplace and repo.

00:38:34.740 --> 00:38:39.840
If you go to.net/skills, you'll
find the.NET Skills repo

00:38:39.840 --> 00:38:42.180
and marketplace, and in
here, there's a whole bunch

00:38:42.180 --> 00:38:44.220
of different plugins that
you can choose to install

00:38:44.220 --> 00:38:45.900
for different.NET scenarios.

00:38:45.900 --> 00:38:50.040
We have these two right here for
ASP.NET Core and for Blazor.

00:38:50.040 --> 00:38:51.810
If you go check out
the Blazor plugin,

00:38:51.810 --> 00:38:53.550
it includes a whole bunch
of brand-new skills

00:38:53.550 --> 00:38:56.790
that just landed recently
for authoring components,

00:38:56.790 --> 00:39:00.390
how to correctly handle user
inputs, configuring auth,

00:39:00.390 --> 00:39:02.610
coordinating components,
a whole bunch of stuff.

00:39:02.610 --> 00:39:06.060
One that's really interesting
is this plan UI change.

00:39:06.060 --> 00:39:08.760
This skill really helps
the agent sort of break

00:39:08.760 --> 00:39:14.130
down the user interface
into composite parts.

00:39:14.130 --> 00:39:15.720
We find that with laser --

00:39:15.720 --> 00:39:18.420
I mean, maybe this is true with
other web UI frameworks as well,

00:39:18.420 --> 00:39:19.560
agents have a tendency to sort

00:39:19.560 --> 00:39:22.200
of create big monolithic
components instead

00:39:22.200 --> 00:39:25.080
of breaking them down
into subcomponents.

00:39:25.080 --> 00:39:26.910
This skill helps
instruct the agent

00:39:26.910 --> 00:39:28.440
so it does a better job of that.

00:39:28.440 --> 00:39:30.540
Let me show you an
example of a test

00:39:30.540 --> 00:39:32.400
that I ran using this skill.

00:39:32.400 --> 00:39:35.070
So here I asked the agent
to just create a Blazor app

00:39:35.070 --> 00:39:41.130
that is a Kanban board, where
you have cards that you can move

00:39:41.130 --> 00:39:44.730
between different columns to
track the progress of work.

00:39:44.730 --> 00:39:48.630
And in the original
case where I ran this,

00:39:48.630 --> 00:39:51.660
I ended up with a board
implementation that looks

00:39:51.660 --> 00:39:55.350
like this, and I think
it's like, a good, like,

00:39:55.350 --> 00:39:57.180
three four hundred
lines of code all

00:39:57.180 --> 00:39:59.280
in one giant board.razor file.

00:39:59.280 --> 00:40:01.170
So not great factoring.

00:40:01.170 --> 00:40:02.310
It does work.

00:40:02.310 --> 00:40:03.420
Let me make sure I've
got it selected.

00:40:03.420 --> 00:40:07.540
So here's the original
app actually functioning.

00:40:08.670 --> 00:40:11.130
So here's the board and you
can see stuff that's to-do,

00:40:11.130 --> 00:40:12.030
in progress, and done.

00:40:12.030 --> 00:40:13.560
You can move stuff left
and right in here,

00:40:13.560 --> 00:40:16.590
a very basic Kanban
board implementation,

00:40:16.590 --> 00:40:19.110
but the factoring of the
components isn't great.

00:40:19.110 --> 00:40:22.710
So then I added the Blazor
plugin and asked the agent

00:40:22.710 --> 00:40:25.530
to build a Kanban app, but
this time, using that,

00:40:25.530 --> 00:40:28.350
the set of Blazor skills
that we've added and ended

00:40:28.350 --> 00:40:31.650
up with something that
now looks like this.

00:40:31.650 --> 00:40:34.500
So here's the updated
version of the application,

00:40:34.500 --> 00:40:37.260
and let's look at
the board page.

00:40:37.260 --> 00:40:38.610
So here's the
refactored version,

00:40:38.610 --> 00:40:40.980
and now you can see I get
some nice subcomponents

00:40:40.980 --> 00:40:42.000
with component parameters

00:40:42.000 --> 00:40:44.610
where we're reflowing data
throughout the application,

00:40:44.610 --> 00:40:47.270
much better factored.

00:40:47.270 --> 00:40:50.330
If I run this one, it's
not exactly the same.

00:40:50.330 --> 00:40:51.620
It was a different agent run,

00:40:51.620 --> 00:40:53.300
so the styling is
slightly different,

00:40:53.300 --> 00:40:55.440
but still just as functional.

00:40:55.440 --> 00:40:58.290
So more idiomatic code,
leveraging the skills

00:40:58.290 --> 00:41:01.680
that we provide in
the.NET Skills repo.

00:41:01.680 --> 00:41:03.600
But definitely check those
out, install the skills

00:41:03.600 --> 00:41:05.580
that are most relevant to you.

00:41:05.580 --> 00:41:11.130
They are added on a need-only
basis so you don't have to worry

00:41:11.130 --> 00:41:13.680
about them contaminating
your context window,

00:41:13.680 --> 00:41:16.920
and we heavily test them using
eval test suites to make sure

00:41:16.920 --> 00:41:21.220
that they have real benefits
for real-world scenarios.

00:41:27.150 --> 00:41:31.080
All right, so that's
what we're delivering

00:41:31.080 --> 00:41:34.950
for.NET web
developers in.NET 11.

00:41:34.950 --> 00:41:36.330
Here's the big picture.

00:41:36.330 --> 00:41:38.670
We're strengthening the
foundation of the platform

00:41:38.670 --> 00:41:41.670
by giving you more performance,
security, and observability.

00:41:41.670 --> 00:41:44.160
These are improvements that
are often driven by some

00:41:44.160 --> 00:41:46.230
of the largest.NET
services in the world,

00:41:46.230 --> 00:41:49.230
and they flow straight into
your apps when you upgrade.

00:41:49.230 --> 00:41:52.500
We're investing in our modern
stack, minimal API, SignalR,

00:41:52.500 --> 00:41:55.320
and Blazor so that you
get awesome new features

00:41:55.320 --> 00:41:57.480
and innovations like
support for C-Sharp unions,

00:41:57.480 --> 00:42:02.450
async validation, OpenAPI
improvements, and so on.

00:42:02.450 --> 00:42:04.400
For Blazor specifically,
we're really focusing

00:42:04.400 --> 00:42:09.020
on server-side rendering
feature parity with MVC as well

00:42:09.020 --> 00:42:14.480
as server scalability and also
the new core CLR-based.NET

00:42:14.480 --> 00:42:16.130
WebAssembly runtime.

00:42:16.130 --> 00:42:18.500
We're making distributed
apps easier with Aspire,

00:42:18.500 --> 00:42:21.770
including better support
for Blazor WebAssembly,

00:42:21.770 --> 00:42:24.650
with service discovery,
end-to-end observability,

00:42:24.650 --> 00:42:26.720
and a production-grade
hosting story

00:42:26.720 --> 00:42:28.190
with the new Blazor gateway.

00:42:28.190 --> 00:42:31.260
We're positioning.NET for
the agentic web together

00:42:31.260 --> 00:42:33.510
with our friends on the
Microsoft Agent Framework team,

00:42:33.510 --> 00:42:35.850
including adding support
for the AGUI protocol

00:42:35.850 --> 00:42:39.420
and Blazor AI components,
and we're making AI smarter

00:42:39.420 --> 00:42:43.080
about.NET with agentic
skills, custom agents, tools,

00:42:43.080 --> 00:42:46.910
and eval test suites so that AI
assistants just love working

00:42:46.910 --> 00:42:48.410
with.NET code.

00:42:48.410 --> 00:42:51.410
And throughout it all,
we're working with you

00:42:51.410 --> 00:42:56.280
to address those top
feedback and pain points.

00:42:56.280 --> 00:42:58.260
Here are some ways that you can
get involved with the future

00:42:58.260 --> 00:43:00.030
of the agentic web today.

00:43:00.030 --> 00:43:01.920
You can try out our.NET
11 previews by going

00:43:01.920 --> 00:43:04.860
to get.dot.net/11,
install the SDK,

00:43:04.860 --> 00:43:06.690
try out one of our new
templates, kick the tires,

00:43:06.690 --> 00:43:08.400
let us know how it goes.

00:43:08.400 --> 00:43:13.530
You can get going with.NET AI at
dot.net/ai, where you can try

00:43:13.530 --> 00:43:16.560
out Microsoft.Extensions.AI,
Microsoft Agent Framework,

00:43:16.560 --> 00:43:20.430
the MCP C-Sharp SDK for
building agentic applications.

00:43:20.430 --> 00:43:25.200
You can Aspire-fy your.NET
apps by going to aspire.dev.

00:43:25.200 --> 00:43:27.060
You can try installing
the new.NET skills

00:43:27.060 --> 00:43:28.680
at the dotnet/skills repo.

00:43:28.680 --> 00:43:31.070
And please build something cool.

00:43:31.070 --> 00:43:32.330
Share with us your experience

00:43:32.330 --> 00:43:35.510
that we can keep making
the platform better.

00:43:35.510 --> 00:43:36.650
Thank you for listening.

00:43:36.650 --> 00:43:39.140
I hope you enjoyed
learning about the future

00:43:39.140 --> 00:43:41.040
of the agentic web with.NET.

00:43:41.040 --> 00:43:43.810
And with that, happy coding.
